The '''colonial expansion of Burawa''' encompasses the period from the founding of [[Lacashe]] in 1516 to the conclusion of the [[War of the Mesogean]] circa 1620. Over approximately a century, the [[Kingdom of Burawa]] transformed from a peninsular power with a single overseas outpost into a colonial empire with territorial possessions in [[Abyala]] and along the northern coast of [[Kaftia]]. The period concluded with a significant strategic reversal: the loss of [[Kingdom of Reige|Reige]] to the [[United Kingdoms of Volisania]] and the effective end of Burawi ambitions in the [[Mesogean Sea]].

Three kings governed during the period. '''[[Dalgayan III]]''' (r. 1491–1524) of [[Kadim-Galwa]] oversaw the founding of [[Lacashe]] and the initial establishment of [[Burawi Cusub]]. '''[[Faarax III]]''' (r. 1524–1563), also of Kadim-Galwa, negotiated the [[Abyala Charter]] with [[Volisanians|Volisania]] and initiated the colonisation of [[Burawi North Kaftia|North Kaftia]]. '''[[Iacob IV]]''' (r. 1563–1608) of [[Kadim-Kaysoor]], who acceded after the Galwa line failed to produce an heir, presided over the conquest of the [[Tapuya Confederacy]] and the [[Aruanãyára Empire]] but was held responsible for the loss of Reige in the War of the Mesogean.

== Lacashe and Burawi Cusub (1516–1540s) ==

=== Early colonial settlement ===
{{main|Lacashe}}

The colony of [[Lacashe]], established by the navigator [[Guled Badweyn]] in 1516 on the eastern coast of [[Abyala]], developed during the first decades into a permanent settlement centred on the southeastern "hump" of the continent. The colony occupied a coastal strip east of the [[Matarrañya-Cañadapirén Range]], the mountain chain that ran along Abyala's eastern seaboard. The initial settlement comprised a fortified trading post, a harbour, a [[Burawi Catholic Church|Church]] mission, and supporting agricultural land.

The settlers encountered indigenous peoples at the intersection of two distinct political systems. To the west of the mountains, in the interior lowlands, lay the [[Tapuya Confederacy]], a complex of tributary chiefdoms with extensive trade networks. Further north along the mountain range existed the [[Aruanãyára Empire]], a highland polity of greater organisational complexity. The coastal zone where Lacashe was founded lay at the margins of trade between these two systems; neither claimed direct sovereignty over the coast, a circumstance that facilitated the initial Burawi settlement.

=== Colonial society ===

The settler population of Lacashe was heterogeneous in composition. Soldiers garrisoned the fortifications and provided security against indigenous incursions. Farmers cleared and cultivated land for plantation agriculture, initially producing crops for local consumption and subsequently for export. Merchants established trading houses linked to the commercial networks of [[Mazar]] and [[Samara]]. [[Burawi Catholic Church|Church]] missionaries operated parishes and schools. The crown also transported convicts to the colony, where they served sentences of forced labour before receiving land grants upon completion of their terms.

The colonial economy was oriented toward the extraction of gold from alluvial deposits in the foothills of the [[Matarrañya-Cañadapirén Range]] and the cultivation of plantation crops — principally sugar, tobacco, and cotton — on the fertile coastal lowlands. The demand for labour on these plantations was a primary driver of the [[Kaftian slave trade]] that expanded throughout the period.

Relations with the indigenous coastal peoples were initially commercial rather than military. The settlers traded manufactured goods — iron tools, textiles, and glass — for food, knowledge of the terrain, and access to interior trade routes. As the colony expanded and plantation agriculture consumed larger areas of land, these relationships deteriorated. By the mid-16th century, the displacement of coastal communities and the enslavement of indigenous labourers had become sources of sustained conflict.

=== The Abyala Charter (1537) ===
{{main|Abyala Charter}}

By the 1530s, both [[Kingdom of Burawa|Burawa]] and [[Volisanians|Volisania]] had established territorial claims on [[Abyala]]: Burawa on the eastern coast, Volisania in the north and west through its colony of [[Balisca]]. Overlapping claims and commercial disputes threatened to produce conflict between the two powers.

In 1537, the two kingdoms concluded the '''[[Abyala Charter]]''', a bilateral treaty that divided the continent between them. The [[Matarrañya-Cañadapirén Range]] served as the principal boundary: territories east of the mountains were assigned to Burawa, territories west to Volisania. The charter also established exclusive trade zones and mutual recognition of existing settlements. The division proved durable; the colonial borders remained essentially unchanged for the remainder of the period.

The charter did not, however, resolve the question of coastal territories south of the mountains, where the range did not extend to the sea. This ambiguity would become a source of dispute in subsequent decades.

=== Growth of Burawi Cusub ===
{{main|Burawi Cusub}}

Under [[Faarax III]] (r. 1524–1563), the Burawi colonial presence in Abyala expanded from the original settlement at [[Lacashe]] northward along the coast. The collective term '''[[Burawi Cusub]]''' ([[Burawi language|Burawi]]: "New Burawa") came into use to describe the totality of Burawi territorial possessions on the eastern seaboard of Abyala, encompassing the original Lacashe settlement and the territories that correspond to modern [[Asikyira]] and portions of [[Balisca]].

Colonial administration was organised through royal governors appointed from [[Samara]], supported by a civil bureaucracy modelled on the peninsular provincial system. The [[Burawi Catholic Church]] operated missions and parishes in parallel, and the [[Cardinal of Burawa]] appointed a bishop for the colonial territories. Towns were founded at harbour sites and river mouths along the coast, connected by a coastal road that facilitated both trade and military movement. By the mid-16th century, Burawi Cusub had developed from a chain of isolated outposts into a continuous colonial territory with a settled agricultural economy, a growing urban population, and institutional links to the metropolitan administration in Samara.

== Colonisation of North Kaftia (~1540s–1580s) ==
{{main|Burawi North Kaftia}}

The second axis of colonial expansion was directed northward along the coast of [[Kaftia]] itself. Burawi forces pushed past [[Ayorin]] into the central body of the continent, but the advance was halted by the [[Tamazgha]] desert, the arid interior that separated [[Southwestern Kaftia]] from the northern and eastern peninsulas.

Unable to penetrate the desert, the crown pivoted to a coastal strategy. Beginning in the 1540s, a chain of military forts and [[Burawi Catholic Church|Church]] missions was established along the northern Kaftian coastline, collectively designated '''[[Burawi North Kaftia]]'''. The principal installations included '''[[Xarunta Badda]]''' ([[Burawi language|Burawi]]: "Sea Command"), a naval base and administrative centre on the northwestern coast; '''[[Dhulka Fog]]''' ("Far Land"), a trading post and provisioning station further north; and '''[[Qalcadda Waqooyi]]''' ("Northern Fort"), the most distant outpost, situated on the coast of the northeastern peninsula.

These installations served three functions: as trading stations for the exchange of goods with northern Kaftian peoples, as provisioning points for ships sailing the transatlantic route to [[Abyala]], and as bases for the growing [[Kaftian slave trade]]. The settlements were not settler colonies in the manner of [[Burawi Cusub]]; they remained primarily military and commercial installations garrisoned by Burawi soldiers and administered by royal officers. The [[Burawi Catholic Church]] established missions at each major fort, directed both at the garrison populations and at the indigenous peoples of the surrounding region.

The North Kaftian forts also functioned as the primary points of embarkation for the slave trade. Enslaved persons captured or purchased in the interior were transported to the coastal installations, held in compounds, and loaded onto ships for the transatlantic crossing to the Abyalan colonies.

== The slave trade ==

=== Development of the triangle trade ===

The institution of slavery, which had expanded during the [[Imperial History of Burawa|Imperial era]], became a central element of the colonial economy during this period. The growth of plantation agriculture in [[Burawi Cusub]] and, to a greater extent, in the [[Volisanians|Volisanian]] colonies of [[Balisca]] and across [[Abyala]] generated sustained demand for enslaved labour.

A '''triangle trade''' developed during the mid-16th century. Enslaved persons were captured or purchased in [[Kaftia]] — through agreements with rulers in [[Ayorin]], [[Imfezzans|Imfezzan]] intermediaries, and northern Kaftian peoples — and transported from the [[Burawi North Kaftia|North Kaftian]] coastal forts and peninsular ports across the [[Aethiopean Ocean]] to the Abyalan colonies. Colonial goods — gold, agricultural products, and tropical commodities — were shipped to the peninsula and to [[Mesogean Sea|Mesogean]] markets. Manufactured goods and trade items were then exported from the peninsula to the Kaftian interior, completing the cycle.

The majority of enslaved persons were transported to [[Abyala]], where the plantation economies of both Burawi and Volisanian colonies absorbed the largest share of the trade. By the end of the 16th century, an estimated 10,000 to 20,000 enslaved persons were shipped from Kaftia to the Abyalan colonies annually. The enslaved population included peoples from the [[Ayorin]] interior, the [[Tamazgha]] desert fringe, the northern Kaftian coastlands, and — increasingly — communities captured in raids conducted by intermediaries operating beyond the direct control of the Burawi state.

=== Institutional support ===

The [[Burawi Catholic Church]], which had sanctioned the enslavement of non-Christians during the [[Imperial History of Burawa|Imperial period]], continued to support the trade during the colonial expansion. Church missionaries accompanied slave ships, baptising enslaved persons during the voyage or upon arrival in the colonies. The theological justification advanced by the Church was that enslavement, while regrettable, facilitated the conversion of non-Christian peoples and was therefore consistent with the Church's missionary mandate.

The crown regulated the trade through a system of licences granted to merchant houses in [[Mazar]] and [[Iskhal]]. The [[Burawi North Kaftia|North Kaftian]] forts served as the primary points of embarkation, though enslaved persons were also shipped from peninsular ports. The trade generated substantial revenue for the crown through export duties and licence fees, and the merchant houses involved in the trade became among the wealthiest in the kingdom.

== Conquest of the Abyalan polities ==

=== The Tapuya Confederacy (~1560s) ===
{{main|Tapuya Confederacy}}

In the 1560s, during the early reign of [[Iacob IV]], Burawi and [[Volisanians|Volisanian]] forces jointly conquered the '''[[Tapuya Confederacy]]''', the complex of tributary chiefdoms that controlled the interior lowlands west of the [[Matarrañya-Cañadapirén Range]].

The Tapuya were a semi-nomadic people who inhabited the vast plains and river valleys of central [[Abyala]]. Their economy was based on seasonal agriculture, hunting, and the management of extensive trade networks that linked the interior lowlands to the highland communities of the [[Matarrañya-Cañadapirén Range|mountain range]] and, through intermediaries, to the coastal zones. The confederacy's political structure was decentralised: autonomous chiefdoms, each controlling a defined territory, were bound by tributary obligations, kinship ties, and reciprocal trade agreements rather than by a centralised administration. Leadership was hereditary within each chiefdom but subject to the approval of neighbouring chiefs through councils that met at seasonal gatherings.

The joint Burawi-Volisanian campaign reflected the cooperative relationship established by the [[Abyala Charter]], with Burawi forces advancing from the eastern coast and Volisanian forces from the west. The confederacy's decentralised structure proved a vulnerability: individual chiefdoms could be isolated, defeated, or induced to defect without the coordinated resistance that a centralised state might have organised. The conquest was completed within the decade, and the former Tapuya territories were divided between Burawi and Volisanian administration. The distribution of territory, however, generated disputes that contributed to the later [[War of the Mesogean]].

=== The Aruanãyára Empire (~1575) ===
{{main|Aruanãyára Empire}}

The '''[[Aruanãyára Empire]]''', a highland polity centred on the northern portion of the [[Matarrañya-Cañadapirén Range]], was conquered by Burawi forces alone in the 1570s. Unlike the joint campaign against the [[Tapuya Confederacy|Tapuya]], this conquest was undertaken without Volisanian participation, as the Aruanãyára territories fell entirely within the eastern zone assigned to Burawa under the [[Abyala Charter]].

The Aruanãyára possessed a more centralised political structure than the Tapuya chiefdoms. The empire was governed by a ruling dynasty seated in a capital of wooden construction in the highland interior. An administrative hierarchy of provincial governors managed outlying territories, and the empire maintained a standing military. Aruanãyára cities were built of timber, adapted to the forested mountain environment, with wooden palisades, elevated granaries, and terraced agricultural plots cut into the hillsides. The empire's wealth derived from control of mountain trade routes that connected the interior lowlands to the eastern coast.

The conquest required sustained military campaigns in mountainous terrain over a period of several years. Burawi forces advanced along river valleys, capturing Aruanãyára settlements and establishing garrisons at strategic points. The centralised structure of the empire, while providing greater capacity for coordinated resistance, also meant that the fall of the capital and the capture of the ruling dynasty brought the war to a decisive conclusion.

The fall of the Aruanãyára Empire extended Burawi control along the full length of the eastern mountain range and secured the colonial interior. It also brought Burawa into direct territorial contact with [[Volisanians|Volisanian]] possessions along the western slopes — a proximity that exacerbated the unresolved boundary disputes arising from the [[Abyala Charter]].

=== The coastal dispute ===

The [[Abyala Charter]] of 1537 had designated the [[Matarrañya-Cañadapirén Range]] as the boundary between Burawi and Volisanian territories. In the interior, where the mountains formed a continuous barrier, this division functioned without difficulty. At the southern extremity of the range, however, the mountains subsided before reaching the coast, leaving a stretch of coastline to which both powers could advance claims. The joint conquest of the [[Tapuya Confederacy]] intensified the dispute: the division of former Tapuya territory along the mountain line left the southern coastal gap unresolved.

The territorial ambiguity, compounded by the separate question of [[Kingdom of Reige|Reige]]'s political allegiance, became a principal cause of the [[War of the Mesogean]].

=== Aftermath for indigenous peoples ===

The conquests of the 1560s and 1570s were devastating for the indigenous populations of eastern [[Abyala]]. The [[Tapuya Confederacy|Tapuya]] chiefdoms, deprived of political autonomy and subjected to colonial administration, experienced rapid demographic decline. The semi-nomadic way of life that had sustained the Tapuya was incompatible with the fixed territorial boundaries imposed by the colonial regime, and the seizure of lands for plantation agriculture displaced communities from their seasonal circuits. Enslaved Tapuya labourers were incorporated into the colonial plantation economy alongside Kaftian enslaved persons shipped across the [[Aethiopean Ocean]]. Communities that escaped colonial control retreated into the deep interior, where remnant populations maintained elements of Tapuya culture beyond the effective reach of colonial authority.

The [[Aruanãyára Empire|Aruanãyára]] experienced a comparable collapse. The fall of the ruling dynasty and the dismantling of the imperial administrative hierarchy removed the institutions that had organised highland society. Aruanãyára cities, built of timber, deteriorated rapidly once their populations were dispersed or conscripted into colonial labour. The mountain trade routes that had sustained the empire's economy were redirected to serve the colonial export trade. The [[Burawi Catholic Church]] established missions in the former Aruanãyára territories, and conversion — voluntary or coerced — replaced the indigenous religious practices within a generation.

By the early 17th century, the indigenous peoples of eastern Abyala had been reduced to a fraction of their pre-contact population. Disease, enslavement, displacement, and the destruction of subsistence systems were the principal causes. The demographic void was filled by the importation of enslaved Kaftian labourers, who became the primary workforce of the colonial economy.

== Volisanian assertiveness under the Lochcarrons (~1580s) ==

The '''[[United Kingdoms of Volisania]]''', formed in 1412 through the union of the [[Kingdom of Volisania]] and the [[Kingdom of Berona]], had been the principal Illypnian colonial power throughout the period. In 1532, the [[House of Lochcarron]] displaced the [[House of Ampuria]] on the Volisanian throne following the [[Ampuria-Lochcarron Wars]]. By the 1580s, the new dynasty had consolidated its authority and embarked on an assertive programme of military modernisation and colonial expansion that altered the balance of power in the [[Mesogean Sea|Mesogean]] region.

The Lochcarron court centralised the colonial administration, expanded the royal navy, and adopted a more aggressive posture toward the territorial disputes arising from the [[Abyala Charter]]. The charter had been signed in 1537 between Burawa and the United Kingdoms under the preceding Ampuria dynasty; the Lochcarrons, while not repudiating the treaty, pursued a broader interpretation of Volisanian rights in [[Abyala]] and the [[Mesogean Sea]].

The new assertiveness brought the question of [[Kingdom of Reige|Reige]] to the fore. Reige, an ethnically [[Volisanians|Volisanian]] island kingdom in personal union with Burawa since the reign of [[Petros II]], had maintained its own lords and institutions for nearly three centuries. The Lochcarron court cultivated the Reigean lords, offering an alternative political alignment based on shared ethnicity and language rather than the dynastic arrangement that bound them to a Kaftian power.

== War of the Mesogean (~1590s–1610s) ==
{{main|War of the Mesogean}}

=== Causes ===

The '''[[War of the Mesogean]]''' arose from the convergence of three disputes: the unresolved southern coastal boundary in [[Abyala]], the political allegiance of [[Kingdom of Reige|Reige]], and the broader assertion of Volisanian power under the [[House of Lochcarron]]. The lords of Reige, cultivated by the Lochcarron court, transferred their allegiance to the [[United Kingdoms of Volisania|United Kingdoms]]. Burawa, which regarded the personal union as a settled constitutional arrangement, refused to accept the defection.

=== Course ===

The war was fought primarily in two theatres: naval engagements in the [[Mesogean Sea]] and the contest for control of [[Kingdom of Reige|Reige]] itself. The Burawi navy, rebuilt since the [[Imperial History of Burawa|Imperial era]] but weakened by the diffusion of resources across the colonial empire, confronted a Volisanian fleet that had been consolidated and modernised under the [[House of Lochcarron]].

The early years of the war were characterised by inconclusive naval skirmishing in the central Mesogean and the interdiction of trade routes between the peninsula and Illypnia. The decisive engagement was the '''[[Battle of Reige]]''', in which the Burawi fleet attempted to retake the island by force. The Volisanian navy, operating from established positions on Reige and supported by the island's lords, defeated the Burawi fleet in a series of engagements in the waters surrounding the island. The loss of the fleet ended Burawa's capacity to project naval power in the Mesogean and rendered the recovery of Reige impracticable.

On the Kaftian front, the [[Kingdom of Orma]], which had maintained its independence through the balance-of-power dynamic between Burawa and the Mesogean states, aligned with [[Volisanians|Volisania]] against Burawa. Orma's commitment to the western front, however, exposed its eastern flank. The '''[[Kingdom of Jarin]]''', an indigenous state east of Orma with significant Mesogean cultural influence, seized the opportunity to invade Orma's eastern territories while the bulk of Orma's forces were engaged against Burawa.

=== Outcome ===

The war concluded with a Burawi defeat. Burawa lost [[Kingdom of Reige|Reige]], which was incorporated into the [[United Kingdoms of Volisania]]. The loss deprived Burawa of its strategic foothold in the central [[Mesogean Sea]] and its principal point of contact with Illypnian trade networks. The disputed southern coastline in Abyala remained unresolved in practice, though the colonial borders established by the [[Abyala Charter]] were otherwise confirmed.

=== Consequences for Burawa ===

The loss of Reige was the most significant territorial reversal since the [[First Orma-Burawa War]] of 1204. The island had served for nearly three centuries as Burawa's gateway to the [[Mesogean Sea|Mesogean]] commercial world: its harbours had provisioned Burawi merchant fleets, its lords had facilitated diplomatic contact with the Illypnian kingdoms, and its position in the central Mesogean had given Burawa strategic leverage disproportionate to the island's size. Without Reige, Burawi access to Illypnian markets was reduced to indirect routes through neutral ports or overland through the [[Triphylian Isthmus]].

The destruction of the fleet at the [[Battle of Reige]] compounded the strategic loss. Rebuilding the navy to Mesogean-projection capability was beyond the kingdom's immediate resources, and the crown instead reoriented the remaining naval capacity toward Atlantic operations: escort of the transatlantic colonial convoys, defence of the [[Burawi North Kaftia|North Kaftian]] forts, and suppression of the piracy that had increased along the [[Aethiopean Ocean]] routes. Burawi ambitions beyond [[Southwestern Kaftia]] and [[Abyala]] were effectively ended.

=== Consequences for the region ===

The war also weakened [[Volisanians|Volisania]]. The prolonged naval commitment in the Mesogean diverted resources from the colonial sphere, and other Illypnian powers — [[Echia]], [[Merica]], [[Breisland]], and [[Aetolia]] — expanded their overseas territories during the distraction. The duopoly that Burawa and Volisania had exercised over [[Abyala]] through the [[Abyala Charter]] was loosened, as the new colonial powers established footholds on coasts and islands that the two principal powers had previously claimed.

On the Kaftian front, the [[Kingdom of Jarin]] gained significant territory at the expense of [[Kingdom of Orma|Orma]]. Jarin's opportunistic invasion of Orma's eastern flank while Orma was committed to the Burawi front resulted in the permanent annexation of a substantial eastern district. This territory would later become the state of '''[[Itegar]]''', established as a buffer territory when the Kingdom of Jarin subsequently divided. The Orma-Jarin conflict reordered the balance of power in southern Kaftia east of Burawa, introducing a new dynamic that persisted into subsequent centuries.

== Dynastic politics ==
{{main|Laanta Boqorka}}

=== Dalgayan III (1491–1524) ===

The colonial expansion period opened under [[Kadim-Galwa]], which had held the throne since the accession of [[Axmed II]] in 1452. '''[[Dalgayan III]]''' (r. 1491–1524), who had overseen the founding of [[Lacashe]] in 1516, devoted the final decade of his reign to the institutional foundations of the colonial enterprise. He expanded the shipbuilding capacity at [[Badalga]] to support the transatlantic route, commissioning a new class of transport vessels adapted to the longer crossing. He appointed the first royal governor of [[Lacashe]], established the system of colonial customs duties that would fund subsequent expansion, and authorised the [[Burawi Catholic Church]] to create a colonial bishopric subordinate to the [[Cardinal of Burawa]]. By the time of his death in 1524, the institutional framework for colonial administration was in place, though the colony itself remained small.

=== Faarax III (1524–1563) ===

'''[[Faarax III]]''' (r. 1524–1563), son of Dalgayan III, inherited a kingdom with a single colonial outpost and transformed it into a multi-territorial empire. His reign saw the negotiation of the [[Abyala Charter]] (1537), the northward expansion of [[Burawi Cusub]], and the establishment of the [[Burawi North Kaftia|North Kaftian]] fort system. These achievements, however, were accompanied by a progressive overextension of royal resources. The simultaneous maintenance of the Abyalan colonies, the North Kaftian garrisons, the peninsular military, and the Mesogean trade networks strained the treasury and the administrative capacity of the state.

Faarax III's solution was to delegate authority: colonial governors were granted broad discretionary powers, the [[Hoggaanka|Hoga]] lords were permitted to raise their own revenues from the colonial trade in exchange for military contributions, and the [[Burawi Catholic Church]] assumed a larger administrative role in the colonies. These measures sustained the expansion during Faarax's lifetime but created structural dependencies that his successors would struggle to manage. His death in 1563 without a male heir ended the Kadim-Galwa line's second period of continuous rule.

=== Transition to Kadim-Kaysoor (1563) ===

[[Faarax III]] died in 1563 without a male heir. The [[Laanta Boqorka]] mechanism functioned without incident: the [[Burawi Catholic Church]] brokered the accession of '''[[Iacob IV]]''' (r. 1563–1608) of [[Kadim-Kaysoor]]. The transition was uncontested. Iacob IV inherited an expansive but overextended kingdom, and his early reign was defined by the attempt to consolidate the territories that Faarax had acquired.

=== The Kaysoor–Galwa crisis (1608) ===

The reign of [[Iacob IV]] was defined by the [[War of the Mesogean]] and the loss of [[Kingdom of Reige|Reige]]. The military and diplomatic failure was attributed to Kaysoor leadership, and Iacob IV's death in 1608 precipitated a succession crisis.

The [[Kadim-Kaysoor]] line produced an heir, but the candidate was a minor. '''[[Cardinal Yusuf]]''', the [[Cardinal of Burawa]] — the single head of the [[Burawi Catholic Church]], who exercised authority equal to but subordinate to the [[Pope]] — aligned himself with [[Kadim-Galwa]] and declared the young Kaysoor heir unfit to govern during a period of crisis. A faction loyal to Kadim-Kaysoor attempted to secure the young king's person, but the plot was discovered and the heir was deposed. A brief civil war ensued between the two branches; Kadim-Galwa, supported by Cardinal Yusuf, the northern [[Hoggaanka|Hoga]] families, and a majority of the senior military commanders, prevailed.

'''[[Nuur III]]''' (r. 1608–1641) of [[Kadim-Galwa]] acceded to the throne. The Galwa branch would hold the kingship continuously for the next century, a monopoly that generated increasing resentment among the [[Kadim-Kaysoor]] and [[Kadim-Mazar]] factions and contributed to the [[Late Colonial History of Burawa|crisis that divided the kingdom in 1709]].

